[{:seon.task/name "a-process-says-what-it-is"
  :seon.task/plan "In Activity Monitor and ps, every process a root forks (the fork server, its standby, each agent, clone and test child) shows the root's own command line, ./seon root PORT, so the owner can't tell a root from an agent or which checkout it belongs to (14 processes across five checkouts, all named alike). Correct: each process names itself by role and checkout when it starts, e.g. \"seon fork-server (next-wiki)\", \"seon agent a3 (next-wiki)\": on macOS by rewriting its argv area in place (what setproctitle does), so ps shows it; the same name is a fact on its entity. Part of the process principle (AGENTS §4). Proof: after spawning an agent, ps -o command= for each pid names its role."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-death-names-its-cause"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: two runs ended `:closed` with reason \"agent a9 died\" and nothing more, while root's log held the causes: an uncaught `{:type :fatal-commit-error}` from Datahike in `seon.repl/record!`, and a ClassCastException (nil cannot be cast to IFn) in `seon.agent/run-tests-forked`, plausibly after the agent redefined a platform function its own loop calls (a real REPL may; the merge judges). The next run and root learn nothing from a bare death. Correct: the agent process's entrant boundary (`seon.agent`'s loop, `seon.fork-server/main` for a child) writes the uncaught exception as the run's end, the one error map (`:seon.error/type`, `:seon.error/operation`, message, cause chain) on its branch, before the process exits; root's judge carries it to the run's reason and evidence. Then find the fatal commit's cause from that evidence."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-step-that-throws-answers-its-error-and-the-agent-goes-on}}
 {:seon.task/name "messages-are-facts"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.agent/messages` takes from the process's :inbox atom; root's :message request appends to it; `my.agent/send-message` goes through root. A message vanishes with the agent, nothing records it, and the owner has no input bar (parity.md 'my.message', '/agent/{id}/message'). v1: messages were facts with received-tx custody, and an unread message woke a turn (~/src/seon/src/my/message.clj). Correct: root writes :seon.message/{id,from,to,body} on the recipient's branch; `my.agent/messages` reads the unreceived ones and records their receipt; an unread message on an idle agent is an event the reconciler sees; the agent page's bar posts one through /call. Deletes: the :inbox atom path for messages (fx replies keep theirs)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.web/call-checks-the-origin"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-message-survives-the-agents-death seon.web-test/the-bar-posts-a-message-fact}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-spawn-says-where-its-time-went"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: root records `:spawned` (with the fork server's `:fork-ms`) and `:ready` (`seon.agent-proc`, `:start-ms` from the agent's `seon.agent/hello!`); nothing in between is visible. So a 30x spawn regression (the memory probe walking a 64 GiB map) showed only as a larger :start-ms and was found by `sample` by hand. Correct: each process stamps `seon.profile/now-ns` (one timeline across processes) at fixed points: root's request, the writer's branch (`seon.writer` :spawn), the fork server's fork, and the child's `seon.db/after-fork!`, connect, ns, memory probe and hello. The child sends its stamps as `:steps` in the hello it already sends, so no new message. Root's agent proc writes one `:spawn-timeline` event per agent, and `bin/check scale` prints p50/p95 per step. Deletes: nothing; replaces reading spawn cost from :start-ms alone."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-spawn-records-each-steps-time}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-boot-interrupt-check-is-read-or-goes"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: every agent boot runs `seon.agent/interrupt-self-test` (agent.clj:265-275): it signals itself SIGUSR1 and evals up to 100,000 times until its handler runs. The result travels in the hello (:interrupt-ok, agent.clj:611 -> repl.clj:406 -> seon.agent-proc :130) and nothing reads it. Correct: a false result is a recorded problem (its owner the agent proc, as a death is), or the check is deleted with its hello key. Deletes the unread key either way."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/an-agent-whose-interrupt-handler-never-ran-is-a-problem}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-reports-its-threads"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed by worktree session agent-view (task seon.render/a-debug-view-of-any-agent, 2026-10-23): the owner asked the agent's page to show its process's threads and uptime. The agent's :stats answer (seon.agent/handle! :stats) and its hello carry pid, memory and t, no thread count; root's view entry has no start stamp (the agent has :seon.agent/started-ms, which the page shows). Correct: the agent reports its OS thread count with its memory (a fact measured, as mem-mb is), root's view keeps it, and the page's head shows it; the head shows the run's start instead of a ticking uptime (a clock deciding a render)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agent-reports-its-threads}}
 {:seon.task/name "fork-error-names-the-threads"
  :seon.task/plan "Done in part: `seon.lib/fork!` forks only while this is the one active Chez thread and otherwise calls its :refused fn at once with the active thread count, so a test run beside a thread answers :seon.test/fork-refused {:active-threads n} (`seon.agent/run-tests-forked`) instead of the old safe-fork's 1 s spin. Left: `seon.agent`'s interrupt-self-test spins up to 100,000 evals waiting for its own SIGUSR1. Correct: the self-test waits on one safe point (one engine tick) and records whether the handler ran. Deletes the 100,000. Its two proofs are not yet written."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-fork-beside-a-thread-is-refused-at-once-naming-it
                      seon.agent-test/the-interrupt-self-test-needs-one-safe-point}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-green-under-the-runner-is-green-in-an-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by a worked example (evaluations session, 2026-10-23): seon.repl-test/an-error-in-an-anonymous-fn-is-in-the-fn-around-it and seon.repl-test/an-eval-error-says-where-in-its-forms-own-text are green under bin/test on main (cb77a911), but red when run with clojure.test/test-vars at any agent's REPL, on main or on a branch (6 assertions: :seon.error/at and :seon.error/in differ, e.g. {:line 1} for {:line 3}, rewrite-clj.zip.seqz/map-seq for nil); the judge's fork of the agent ran the first red too, and the done rule held the example's run for it as a stray red (with seon.schema-test/register-validates-against-the-population). A test whose verdict depends on where it runs judges nothing: an agent's branch is held for a red its change did not cause. Find the difference (the agent loads its program from the branch's recorded forms, the runner from files: an error's position and the frames Jolt reports may differ), fix it at its producer, so a test's verdict in an agent and its fork equals the runner's."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-outside-runs-scratch-stays-scratch"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by a worked example (evaluations session, 2026-10-23; notes example-1 in agent-view's notes folder): to call a test namespace's private helper an agent ran (in-ns 'seon.repl-test) and defined scratch values there (seon.repl-test/changed, eval-code-src, long-bad); the branch recorded them as definitions of that namespace, so its merge would carry them (they showed under :will-merge :added). Nothing an agent can call takes a definition back off its branch: ns-unmap and ```seon.program/remove-definition!``` unmap only in the process. Correct: an agent can undo a definition it made on its branch (recorded as its removal, which the merge carries as one), and the context tells it to work in its own namespace and reach a private helper as @#'ns/f. Proof: an agent that defines a value in a program namespace and removes it leaves nothing of it in what its merge carries."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agents-first-read-costs-what-a-later-one-does"
  :seon.task/plan "A fresh agent's first request build (seon.turn/context-block) cost about 126M ticks on a worktree root of 908 tasks, against 22-33M for a later one, with main's reconciler index handed by the fork server (task seon.turn/the-fork-server-hands-main-s-index): its first observe 50M against about 8M warm, its first render about 40M more than a later one, the same calls each time. The cause was first reads of the store: seon.db/after-fork! forgot the parent's connections and with them their node caches, so the agent's own connect started an empty cache and fetched and decoded every node it read (a first seon.problems/caused-split: 487 misses, 1,436 nodes decoded, 38.9M ticks against 7.2M warm, about 65k a miss). Now after-fork! keeps each node cache alone (decoded values at content addresses), and the agent's first connect adopts it (datahike.connections/reserve-connection-opening! shares a cache by node store and threshold): measured on fresh agents, the first observe 21.6M (3 misses) and the first render no dearer than a later one (33.8M against 32.6M); a larger task's whole first read 79M against 61.5M later. Left: about 14M of the first observe is still first-time work that is no store read (seon.problems/caused-split reads sections, var names and catch forms the fork server never decoded: 20.4M against 4.6M, with base and head equal), and the spawn's own key pull, now gone (root names the key). Not yet proved: that the whole first read costs what a later one does, which needs a process root per size (an agent-test cost proof)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-fork-keeps-its-parents-node-caches-and-no-connection}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-run-streams-its-reports"
  :seon.task/plan "Found proving task seon.proofs/main-is-proved-in-a-fork: the test child of seon.agent/run-tests-forked wrote the run's one result line at its end, so a child's death (the test service's run of all of main's tests: 344 in one child, dying at a varying test with exit 1 and nothing on stderr, a cause not yet found: the Jolt session's fork-child work) lost every report before it: 0 results for 200 s of CPU. Done: run-tests-here writes {:seon.test/ended v :reports [...]} as each test ends; seon.agent/test-child forks one child and reads it (the reports that arrived, the tests that ended, the child's final line or the one :error on the test it died in); run-tests-forked loops, running what has not ended or died in a new child, so a death costs the one test it interrupted (its :error stays, no verdict stored) and not the run. The final line carries only counts, so it no longer grows with the suite. Replaces the single result line. Proof: seon.agent-test/a-death-costs-the-test-it-interrupted-and-no-other (a middle test that exits the child: the others pass, it is the one error). Then done (2026-10-29): the run is dealt into at most :seon.config.proofs/jobs children by seon.proofs/shards (the one deal bin/test uses, moved from test-runner), each read on its own fiber; one child is the one-child case (262 tests: 91.7 s with jobs 1, 48.9 s with jobs 4, proof seon.agent-test/a-test-run-is-dealt-across-children-and-loses-none). Not done: turning :seon.config.proofs/prove-main-on-commit back on."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-death-costs-the-test-it-interrupted-and-no-other}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-prove-answer-names-its-first-failure"
  :seon.task/plan "Dogfood 0.7, form: seon_prove after a red answers {:ran 1 :pass 0 :fail 0 :error 1 :red [seon.task-test/status-answers-before-any-turn] ...}; the reason (\"no process store\", \"test process died\") is only in (:reports (my.agent/prove)), about 60 report maps of every test in the namespace, and one error report says :reused true. (my.task/status) for the same red answers :status :unknown with no failure text. The change, at the producer of the answer (my.agent/prove's result, the server's seon_prove): each :red test is named with its first failing assertion or error message and the form that runs it, as the failures of a status proof are, and the full :reports stays the form that reads the rest. Proof: a prove with one red answers that test's name and message in its first level."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-prove-answer-names-the-red-test-and-its-first-assertion}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-running-fibers-dump-shows-its-current-frames"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-27 (the turn-0 hang, task seon.agent/a-turn-0-prove-does-not-walk-the-checkout): seon's fiber dump (kill -INFO) of a process whose only fiber is running answers 'running fiber, no saved continuation' and names nothing, so the one process that burned 175e9 ticks could not be located in code; its native sample showed only getcwd and directory listing. A running fiber's current frames must be in the dump (Jolt's runtime: the fiber's own stack at the signal, as a parked fiber's saved continuation shows), so the dump of a spinning process names the Clojure function it is in. Belongs to Jolt (a jolt/<topic> branch, AGENTS section 1); owner jolt-compare. Proof: a process spinning in a known function (a loop in a test fixture) dumps that function under its running fiber."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-dump-of-a-spinning-process-names-its-function}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-spawns-start-costs-the-same-on-a-bigger-program"
  :seon.task/plan "Red on main before the 2026-10-28 batch and taken out of seon.agent-test then (the owner's rule: a red of unknown cause the batch did not make leaves its test, named here): an agent spawned after main gained 200 forms has a :state phase of 616,608 bytes against 460,304 before, over the bound 1.1x + 64 KiB. A spawn's own work must not grow with main's program (the owner, 2026-10-25). Find what the :state phase reads or builds per form (seon.agent/spawn-phases; the ctx record's pull, the reach graph or index handed at fork, the branch views), fix it at the cause, and restore the deftest from git (it is a-spawns-start-costs-the-same-on-a-bigger-program in test/seon/agent_test.clj at 139bbb75ff)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-evals-budget-comes-from-measured-evals"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-30: a budget where 90%+ of the time it's under budget and the rare overrun is mostly a time we should intervene (docs/decisions.md). What exists: an eval's tick and byte budgets are fixed config (:seon.config.agent/form-ticks 20 G, :seon.config.agent/form-bytes 1 GiB), applied by seon.repl to the origins seon.repl/budgeted names (:model, :outside, :ui), counted by seon.intr/run; a test's tick budget is derived from its own history (seon.fx/test-budgets: min(cap, max(floor, factor x the median of its green ticks))). The fixed byte budget stopped root's whole-program loads (fixed: root's evals unbudgeted), an eval's (my.agent/prove) (task seon.agent/a-taken-test-run-is-not-the-forms-work) and seon.agent-test/a-test-childs-ceiling-is-its-own. Plan: (1) measure the population first: every recorded agent eval's :ticks and :bytes by origin, from the md-scan bench store's run branches (main holds only root's own evals: 32 records, 2026-10-30), p50, p90, p99, max; (2) one budget function for tests and evals, budget-of [samples {:quantile :factor :floor :cap}] (the per-test budget is the case quantile 0.5 over one test's greens), seon.fx/test-budgets calling it; (3) an eval's budget per origin is budget-of over the recorded evals of that origin (quantile and factor so that 90%+ of past evals pass, config keys with their provenance), derived where an agent is forked (root, from the population at t; derived data, regenerated when evicted), the fixed keys becoming the cap; (4) an overrun stays the typed error to the agent (:seon.repl/ticks-spent, :seon.repl/bytes-spent) and is recorded as a fact, an intervention point root can show. Open: the budget's unit of scope (per eval now; per fiber once the agent's intake dispatcher runs a request on its own fiber: the context session's measurement of whether Jolt fibers share the per-thread counter). Proofs to write: budget-of's cost and quantile behavior; an eval within the derived budget runs; one past it is the typed error; the test budgets unchanged (same numbers before and after)."
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-agent-is-a-flow-graph"
  :seon.task/plan "Step (1) of THE PLAN's agreed order (docs/plan.md#where-it-stands-2026-10-30-morning): remove the hang class. The owner, 2026-10-30: move more things to Flow, no hand-written ways of controlling processes, Clojure's and its libraries' words. What exists: the agent reads root's socket by hand (seon.agent/intake, a fiber; recv-raw!, poll!, next-msg!), and several waits take from it (the idle loop under seon.step/run-loop, seon.agent/await-op! inside prove!, fx! and clone!, seon.agent/inbox), each putting what it does not want on a shared :pending queue; a :tests request is handled by whichever wait takes it (*waiting*, take-op, answered-anyway!, and the ::say/::ran handoff in seon.agent/handle!). md-scan #21 hung on a :tests left in :pending, #26 on one abandoned when its eval's byte budget stopped it. Root's side is already a Flow proc on a jolt.async.remote end (seon.agent-proc/agent-proc). Measured in scratch Jolt processes, 2026-10-30: a Flow graph of :io procs on the main carrier stays one active thread through start, inject, ping and stop, and forks at every point; a fiber's allocation is not charged to a parked eval's byte budget (the same allocation in the eval stops it at 10.88 MB of 10 MB); an eval blocked in one proc's step, waiting on a promise, gets its answer while another proc serves root's nested request; an interrupt stops an eval parked on a promise or a channel (206 ms after its token was set); ping leaves a proc that is inside a step out and the graph does not wedge. Research: one reader routing replies by id and requests apart (nREPL's :id, LSP's every request answered even when cancelled, Erlang's monitor answering a dead callee's caller); Flow has no request/reply primitive, a request carries its reply-to [pid in-port] (the Flow guide). The change, in steps, each its own commit with a test watched failing first: (1) the agent's frames come to it on one channel and leave on one: today's reader fiber (seon.agent/intake, seon.sock/recv!, which takes a descriptor sent with a frame: the clone's socket, seon.agent/clone!) is the :transport proc's in-port, and every proc sends its frames whole itself (a write blocks the thread, so no two frames interleave); a jolt.async.remote end (as root's is) once Jolt's end carries a descriptor with a value (task jolt.async/a-remote-end-carries-descriptors; its reader recv(2) drops SCM_RIGHTS today), which deletes seon.sock's agent side; serve! runs a Flow graph of :io procs, :transport (the end's :in is its in-port; routes requests by :op, answers by :id) and :repl (agent-step's evals, :judge, :merge-main, :render, one at a time), in place of seon.step/run-loop; root's :pause, :resume, :stop and :ping frames map onto Flow's own; the lib/macos? gate in seon.repl/run! goes (one loop on every platform; the Linux gaps are Jolt's: jolt.io-poller/wake-on-signals-on-linux, jolt.async/a-forked-child-closes-inherited-ends-on-linux); measured: an agent's ready time and memory against main's (ten agents ready in 172 ms). (2) a :tests proc serves root's :tests on its own fiber (plan, fork the child, read its pipe, answer); prove! injects its request with a promise and derefs it; deletes *waiting*, take-op, answered-anyway!, ::say and ::ran. (3) every wait (fx!, the clone's :fd and :branched, inbox) is a promise the transport holds by :id; the agent's own requests carry :id, not :k; at the end's close every promise held is delivered :dead; deletes :pending, next-msg!, poll!, recv-raw! and await-op!'s loop. (4) a step that throws reaches Flow's error channel and its message is answered by its :id with the error (seon.root/answer-taken's pattern, reused). (5) seon.turn/work! checks :seon.agent/max-turns once, after the green check and before any model call; a turn that threw goes round. (6) measured against main: src lines, a prove's ticks and bytes, ready time and memory; then md-scan run 28 on a main of its own (SEON_MAIN=bench-28). Wiki: docs/processes.md (the agent process, the socket and the agent proc), docs/flow.md (Flow inside an agent), docs/failure-points.md, docs/turn.md, docs/clocks.md (Flow's stop and ping bounds in agents). Adapted as it is learned: steps (1)-(3) are one change (with only a transport, the waits inside an eval would still read the same channel); step (4), the owner's ruling of 2026-10-30: a step that throws answers its request with the error and the agent goes on (seon.repl/defect-answer), where it ended the process with exit 70 and the run :error (seon.agent/died!, deleted). Measured 2026-10-30 on jolt/agent-flow: src +185 -239 (net -54); a three-proc graph starts in 0.06-0.17 ms; a prove of a changed branch while an eval blocks answers in 64 ms (main: it waits behind the eval for good); (my.agent/prove) under a 30 MB byte budget passes while the run allocates 60 MB on the thread."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-tests-request-is-answered-while-prove-waits seon.agent-test/a-tests-run-is-not-charged-to-the-eval-that-waits seon.agent-test/a-stopped-evals-tests-request-is-still-answered seon.agent-test/root-gone-ends-every-wait seon.agent-test/a-step-that-throws-answers-its-error-and-the-agent-goes-on seon.agent-test/every-turn-throwing-ends-at-the-cap}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-child-that-dies-says-why"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-30 in four full proves (batch 48's, ratom-teach's twice, and one more): bin/check scale fails because 2 to 4 of its 100 agents answer their test run {:pass 1 :fail 0 :error 1 :test 1 :reports []}: an error with no report, the child's exit 0, its heap ~337 MB (the :heap of f-test 336-337 MB every time); green in three other full proves the same day. A test child that errors and reports nothing breaks the principle that every failure comes back as data naming its cause (AGENTS section 4, nothing crashes and nothing hangs). Correct: find what the error is (the child's stderr, its exit and signal, its heap against :seon.config.agent/trip-bytes and the inherited bytes; the 337 MB heap of a one-assertion test is itself the first suspect), make the child's death or refusal a report with its cause, then fix the cause. Evidence: re-run bin/check scale under load (another prove beside it reproduced it), keep its scale.log. Found since (batch 49 made the report reach its asker, seon.proofs/answer): the error is :seon.test/pipe-held, \"the test process exited and a process it forked still holds its pipe (its end of file never came)\" (batch 51's prove, 1 of 100 agents). So a process other than the test child holds the pipe's write end: one the agent forked at the same moment (a sibling fork, or a spawned command without close-on-exec) inherits it. Next: name the holder in the report (the pipe's other holders, from the fd's inode, when it is held), then close the write end in every fork but the child's (the fds atom run-all keeps covers only test children, and only once each is registered)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-test-child-that-dies-reports-why}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-reader-of-an-agents-branch-sees-what-its-answered-evals-wrote"
  :seon.task/plan "seon.repl-test/an-edit-in-an-agent-is-program-on-its-branch went red once in batch 66's merge prove (2026-10-31, at ff5f74f128; a 44-test shard of process tests) and green alone twice at the same tree: the agent answered its edit! (:edited), then root's eval read the agent's branch (seon.root/branch-db) and its :var/form for the edited var held the old source, \"[] 2\" missing. So an eval's answer can reach its asker before the branch commit that holds what the eval wrote is readable by another process (seon.repl's serve loop commits after the answer when :dirty?, agent/commit!). AGENTS section 5: a write is answered when committed in memory, and one followed by an outside effect waits for the sync that covers it; an outside reader acting on an answer is such an effect. Reproduce first: repeat the test's three steps in one shard with load beside it, and count. Correct: what an answered eval wrote is on the branch any reader opens after the answer (commit before answering, or the answer carries the commit and branch-db waits for it); no wait by clock."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-edit-in-an-agent-is-program-on-its-branch}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-child-starts-as-a-test-runner-does"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-02 by the context lab's curation pilot (a scripted replay on a reverse-patched tree; report in /private/tmp/seon-l2r/618a903ed3/tree/target/curated-turn-5.edn): the agent's prove passed its proof but 13 reached tests failed and 3 errored in its test child, all green under bin/test in the same tree, so the run parked :waiting and no change near seon.ctx, seon.repl or seon.db could end green. Three causes: (1) root's interrupt pending in the agent when the turn's observe forked its test child (it is cleared only when the turn's evals begin, seon.turn/act!), so every eval in the child answered \"interrupted by root\"; (2) the child kept the agent's live page walk, whose reactions answered a test's swap! of ctx (seon.ctx-test/a-swap-costs-what-it-changed 128 calls against 113); (3) seon.db-test's corpus read docs/ relative to the working directory, which in a test child is its own run directory. Correct: a test child starts as bin/test's runner does, its interrupt state cleared (seon.intr/forked!), proven; a test reads the checkout's files by (seon.config/checkout). Cause (2) is unconfirmed: a child forked from inside an eval held no walk (seon.agent-test/a-test-child-starts-as-a-test-runner-does), so the cost overrun is reproduced from the pilot's report before anything is changed for it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
